Troy Goode wrote most of the code that survives today, and Douglas Wilson is a close second. By surviving lines, Troy's share is about 43% and Douglas's is about 33%. Troy leads even though Douglas has more commits (143 to 120).

I counted lines with `git blame` across the 19 tracked files, excluding lockfiles and images. I used `-w -M -C` so whitespace changes and moved lines don't take credit. I also used `.git-blame-ignore-revs` so the 4-space/double-quote reformat commit (`62628c0`) doesn't hand every line to whoever ran it.

| Author | Surviving lines | Share (of ~1,900) |
|---|---|---|
| Troy Goode (`<email redacted>`) | 762 | ~40% |
| Troy Goode (`troy@laptop.example`, a second email) | 48 | ~3% |
| Douglas Wilson | 629 | ~33% |
| Iñigo Marquínez | 95 | ~5% |
| Jon Church | 72 | ~4% |
| Sebastian Beltran | 67 | ~3.5% |
| Ulises Gascón | 52 | ~3% |
| Ignacio Mangas | 44 | ~2% |
| Alejandro Caravaca Puchades | 34 | ~2% |
| About 6 others | 10–26 each | the rest |

- **Troy's two emails:** I combined them to get the 43%. Raw blame counts would split his lines across the two addresses.
- **The repo's history:** It runs from 2013-01-30 to 2026-04-12, and Troy made the first commit. That is why so much of his original code is still there.
- **What I haven't checked:** Whether Troy or anyone else is still active. That matters if you want to know who can still review this code, not just who wrote it. I haven't run the busfactor tool, which weights recent work more heavily and flags areas whose authors have left. I can run it (read-only, with `--dry-run --no-github`) and give you per-directory ownership and orphaned zones. Want me to?

